// @run
// @log 5
// @log 6
// @hint redundant type annotation: `q` is inferred as `{a: i32, b: i32}[]`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `w` is inferred as `{xs: AB[]}`
// AN INTERSECTION ALIAS'S REF LIST ASSIGNED INTO A STRUCTURALLY-SPELLED FIELD LIST
// (silent-class-inventory D442). Was check-clean INVALID WASM — and the second HINT above
// is the point: the checker calls `{xs: AB[]}` and `W` the same type, while the emitter
// gave the two element spellings separate heap types and therefore separate ref-list
// WRAPPERS (`(ref 4)` over `(array (mut (ref null 0)))` for the field, `(ref 6)` over
// `(array (mut (ref null 1)))` for `q`), with no coercion between them. Same root as D441:
// the alias row could not twin with the inline shape's row.
//
// THE POSITION IS WHY THIS ROW EXISTED SEPARATELY. Measured over sixteen structurally-spelled
// positions, D402's loud `checkArrName` reject reached TWELVE and missed four: a struct field
// of the inline shape's list read, the same written, an un-annotated list — all three correct
// — and this one, the same field fed from an ALIAS-typed binding. So the boundary had four
// cells and exactly one of them was silent: a loud reject's boundary is where its family's
// silent cells sit. All three ingredients were removable alone: a plain-struct alias runs,
// a field spelled `AB[]` runs, and an inline literal with no intermediate binding runs.
type AB = {a: i32} & {b: i32}
type W = { xs: {a: i32, b: i32}[] }
const q: AB[] = [{ a: 5, b: 6 }]
const w: W = { xs: q }
print(w.xs[0].a)
print(w.xs[0].b)
